  "Question": "A function is both odd and even, and it is given that \\(f(3)=0\\). What should \\(f(0)\\) be?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "0",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "If an odd function is defined at 0, then \\(f(0)=-f(0)\\), so \\(f(0)=0\\). A function that is both odd and even can only be identically 0 on its domain, since \\(f(x)=f(-x)=-f(x)\\).\n\nHowever, a domain symmetric about 0 need not contain 0. For example, \\(f(x)=0\\) on \\(\\{-3,3\\}\\) is both odd and even and satisfies \\(f(3)=0\\), but \\(f(0)\\) is undefined. Thus the answer is \\(0\\) if \\(0\\) belongs to the domain; without that assumption, the question does not guarantee that \\(f(0)\\) exists.",
